<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Basic Law for the Federal Republic of Germany</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1949">1949</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Grundgesetz</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Basic Law for the Federal Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> is the German constitution, drafted by a parliamentary council of the western states and in force from 23 May 1949. Intended as provisional until reunification, it set up a federal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliamentary-democracy">parliamentary democracy</a> with strong basic rights and a constitutional court. It became the constitution of all Germany in 1990. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>'s Landtag rejected it but accepted its validity.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/basic-law-for-the-federal-republic-of-germany.jpg" alt="Basic Law for the Federal Republic of Germany" width="255" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: bpb.de · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grundgesetz.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Between February and June 1948 the London Six-Power Conference, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, the United Kingdom and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a>, debated the political future of the three western occupation zones of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and concluded that a democratic and federal West German state should be established. The West German constitution was adopted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bonn">Bonn</a> on 8 May 1949, approved by the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> on 12 May and came into effect on 23 May. It was called the Grundgesetz, Basic Law or Fundamental Law, rather than a Verfassung, constitution, because its drafters regarded it as an interim arrangement for a provisional state, pending a definitive constitution for a reunified Germany. It was never put to a popular vote.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The authors sought to ensure that a dictator could never again come to power. The first article protects human dignity, and the fundamental rights of Articles 1 to 19 bind all state authority, whereas the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar-constitution">Weimar Constitution</a> had listed them only as state objectives. Article 20 sets out the principles of democracy, republicanism, social responsibility, federalism and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rule-of-law">rule of law</a>. Under the eternity clause, Article 79 (3), Articles 1 and 20 cannot be amended. The original text had no emergency powers like those of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag-fire-decree">Reichstag Fire Decree</a> of 1933, and it allows a right of resistance against anyone seeking to abolish the constitutional order.</p><p>It set up a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliamentary-democracy">parliamentary democracy</a> with separation of powers. The Federal President is head of state and the Federal Chancellor head of government, who can be dismissed only through a constructive vote of no confidence. The Bundestag is elected by proportional representation, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bundesrat-germany">Bundesrat</a> represents the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/states-of-germany">Länder</a>, and the Federal Constitutional Court guards the Basic Law. Unlike <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a>'s, the text names political parties and allows the court to abolish those that violate its democratic foundations.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The Basic Law offered two routes to unity: Article 23, under which other parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> could declare their accession, and Article 146, under which a new constitution could be adopted by the German people. Reunification in 1990 used Article 23, and the Basic Law was kept as the definitive constitution of the united country. Article 23 was then repealed, to reaffirm the renunciation of any claim to land east of the Oder and Neiße, and an article on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> was inserted instead. The Basic Law was amended in 1955 to allow federal armed forces, the Bundeswehr, and in 1968 to introduce emergency competencies. Some interpreters see it as an attempt to correct Germany's supposed Sonderweg, or special path.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Basic Law for the Federal Republic of Germany” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>West Germany was established as a federal parliamentary republic with a social market economy under the <b>Grundgesetz</b>, the Basic Law of 1949.</blockquote><a href="/empire/federal-republic-of-germany">Federal Republic of Germany · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Bavaria joined the Federal Republic of Germany in 1949, though its Landtag rejected the <b>Basic Law</b> while accepting its validity.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-state-of-bavaria">Free State of Bavaria</a></li></ul></section>
